//! check: fail(E1001)
//! phase: typecheck
//! conforms: mem.tier0.move.2, mem.model.place
//!
//! s182 (wolf-lang#446, option A kept: "element moves are one place
//! on the compiler and per-element on lupin") — `move xs[0]` on a
//! `List[int]` and then a read of `xs[1]`. `Proj::Opaque` collapses
//! every element of a container to ONE place — element granularity is
//! a declared non-target (s18) — so the move of `xs[0]` empties `xs[_]`
//! and the read of `xs[1]` is E1001 on both wolfgang lanes. 0.2.14
//! already refused the same program for a non-`Copy` element type;
//! s177's #444 fix (record the move whatever the type) widened it to
//! `Copy` elements, which is where this row pins it.
//!
//! lupin 0.1.38 models elements individually and RUNS this (`1 2`,
//! `exit(0)`): a documented conservatism divergence, the same class as
//! `[mem.tier0.mode.read]`'s rows in the interpreter's divergence log —
//! the compiler refuses a program the language does not forbid, and
//! says so here rather than leaving the next reader to find it. The
//! driver test `element_move_conservatism_lanes.rs` asserts both sides.

fn main() -> !int {
    var xs = List[int]()
    (mut xs).push(1)
    (mut xs).push(2)
    let a = move xs[0]
    print("{a} {xs[1]}")           // ERROR E1001: `xs[_]` is one place; `xs[0]` was moved
    0
}
